Z||000015||Formula88||08-16-2001||11:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>You remember the saying, if it sounds to good to be true, it probably is?  Well that applies to these bank interest schemes people think of when dreaming of winning the lottery.  <P>If you were to have 30 mil. sitting in your hands, you can't just walk into a bank and say "yeah I wanna open up a savings account".  You will have to set up a special meeting with all kinds of chairmen and VIPs about putting your money in their bank.  The chances of getting a 5% interest rate is next to impossible.  Remember, they do this for a living, they aren't dumb, they know what you could make off their interest too.  You would be lucky to get 1-2%.  Not to mention you would probably have to hire a lawyer to be on the safe side or to write up some type of contract, he will cost you some money too.  Then don't forget about the Gold Club!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, most any bank would be falling all over you to take your money and pay you 5%.  That gives them the 120M to loan out to other people at 10%-20%.  The interest they pay you is nothing compared to what they make off your money.  Now, if you had 120M in the bank and wanted to withdraw all of it?  THAT's when you need the special meetings, cuz they've likely got a lot of it invested.  That would represent a large chunk of many bank's liquid assets.  And they wouldn't want to loose that.  I imagine they'd offer you MORE interest than normal, not less, simply because of the money they stand to gain by having your deposit.  Don't for a minute think a bank is doing you a favor by paying you interest on your money.  If it wasn't profitable for them, they wouldn't do it.||||reg||1
